  "teaches": [
    "noun-class pairs 1/2, 7/8, and 9/10",
    "obligatory noun-class prefixes before noun stems",
    "subject, tense/aspect, optional object, stem, and final-vowel verb slots",
    "separate personal and noun-class feature structures",
    "parser work and grammar ambiguity"
  ],
  "summary": "A synthetic parser exercise demonstrates cited Bantu-style noun-class prefixes, a fixed verb template, and ambiguity from duplicated optional object-marker slots.",
  "disclaimer": "SYNTHETIC EXAMPLE. This language data was generated to demonstrate Motif. It is modelled loosely on Swahili, but it is not real Swahili, has not been checked by speakers, and must not be used as a description of any language.",
  "description": "SYNTHETIC EXAMPLE. This language data was generated to demonstrate Motif. It is modelled loosely on Swahili, but it is not real Swahili, has not been checked by speakers, and must not be used as a description of any language. The grammar models only the cited 1/2, 7/8, and 9/10 noun-class pairs and the verb template SUBJECT – TENSE/ASPECT – (OBJECT) – STEM – FINAL VOWEL. It uses no productive phonological rules. The constructed Texts combine cited word forms for parser exercises; they are not speaker-checked sentences or evidence of clause-level agreement. The written n- plus dizi split is a teaching analysis, not a productive nasal rule.",
